Gaining levels gets *you* hit points, as well.  It also increases your
hit point recovery, thereby reducing your downtime and hence your food
consumption.  Furthermore, if you do reach level 30, further "gain
level" effects improve your HP and Pw without making the monsters any
more powerful.

The difference in monster hit points between 14th and 30th player
experience level is (4*4.5=) 18 hit points for monsters weaker than
14th level.  For 15th-18th level monsters, it's 13.5 hit points.  For
19th-22nd level monsters, it's 9 hit points.  For 23rd-26th level
monsters, it's 4.5 hit points.  For 27th-30th level monsters, it's
zero.

>Since magic damage does appear to increase with level, would it be
>better for a wizard to go past XL14 to do the extra damage?  It appears
>the extra damage is larger than the extra monster HP, and that the
>monster level doesn't increase fast enough to raise their magic resist
>rate.

Wizards and Knights both definitely want to be 30th level.  At 30th
level, a wizard can dish out 16d6 damage (average damage: 56, which is
more than *any* artifact weapon can possibly deal in melee except a
heavily enchanted Grayswandir against a silver-hater) to each target his
magic missile spells hit; a knight carrying his quest artifact can dish
out 32d6.

(Also, Wizards get best advantage from their utility spells if they've
got lots of skill slots available to spend on raising magic skills.
Your total number of skill slots is based on your level.)

The average damage from 16d6 is greater than the average hit points of a
12th-level monster; a 30th level wizard can therefore flatten a Rider of
the Apocalypse (who attack at 30th level but only have 10d8 hit points)
in a single hit more than 25% of the time.

The average damage from 32d6 is greater than the average hit points of
a *24th* level opponent, so you have roughly a 25% chance of flattening
all but the mightiest foes in one hit. (As far as I can tell, the only
non-unique monster above 24th level is the arch-lich.)

The only monsters that have inherent immunity to magic missiles are
Angels, the Oracle, gray dragons (either size), Yeenoghu, and the
Chromatic Dragon.  Monsters using an item that gives magic resistance
or reflection are also immune to magic missiles.
